---
// Page body converted from the Claude Design handoff (local-service-r3.html). Edit freely.
import { site, u } from '../../data/site';
import FormMeta from '../FormMeta.astro';
---
<div class="hero on-tan hero-c">
<div class="hero-in c-center">
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>Bookkeeping &amp; CFO services &middot; Victoria, BC</p>
<h1 class="h1 hero-h" style="max-width:20ch">Bookkeeping and CFO services for Victoria contractors, from a CPA who has <span class="em">actually worn the boots</span>.</h1>
<p class="lead hero-lead">Kevin Loo, CPA, CMA, runs the books for general contractors and subcontractors across Greater Victoria &mdash; from Langford to Sidney, Sooke to the Saanich Peninsula. One person handles your file every month, not a rotating cast of juniors.</p>
<div class="hero-act">
<a class="tel-lg" href={site.tel}>{site.phone}</a>
<a class="btn btn-1" href={u('/contact/')}>Book a call</a>
<a class="btn-txt" href="#included">See what is included</a>
</div>
<p class="small muted" style="margin:0 auto">No portal, no ticket number. If you call, you get Kevin.</p>
</div>
</div>

<section style="padding:var(--pad-major) 0">
<div class="seg-in">
<div class="split">
<div>
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>Why Victoria contractors call Parallel</p>
<h2 class="h2">Victoria does not need a bigger accounting firm. It needs one CPA who already gets job costing.</h2>
</div>
<div>
<p class="p">Most bookkeepers price by the hour and hand your file to whoever is free that week. Parallel works differently &mdash; one CPA, CMA, working with a fixed roster of contractors on Vancouver Island, who already understands holdbacks, WIP schedules and progress billing before your first invoice lands.</p>
<p class="p">That is the whole pitch: fewer clients, and every one of them gets someone who actually knows their file.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="pts">
<div class="pt"><span class="pt-n">01</span><h3 class="h4">Direct access.</h3><p>Every file gets Kevin&rsquo;s own line, not a support queue.</p></div>
<div class="pt"><span class="pt-n">02</span><h3 class="h4">Built for job sites, not bakeries.</h3><p>Job costing and holdbacks are the starting point, not something learned on your file.</p></div>
<div class="pt"><span class="pt-n">03</span><h3 class="h4">One point of contact, permanently.</h3><p>Nobody gets handed to a junior partway through the year.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<div class="cta on-dark cta-c">
<div class="cta-in">
<div>
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>Get started</p>
<h2 class="h2 cta-h">A scope and a price for your books, before anything moves.</h2>
<p class="cta-p">Tell Kevin what is eating your week &mdash; bookkeeping, payables, receivables, or all of it. He will tell you what it costs and exactly what is included, in writing, before you sign anything.</p>
</div>
<div class="cta-act">
<a class="tel" href={site.tel}>{site.phone}</a>
<a class="btn btn-1" href={u('/contact/')}>Book a call</a>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<section style="padding:var(--pad-major) 0">
<div class="seg-in c-center">
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>What changes</p>
<h2 class="h2" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-5)">Four things that actually change once Parallel takes the books.</h2>
<p class="p">None of these are complicated on their own. They are just the four that a busy contractor stops keeping up with first, and the four Kevin fixes in roughly that order.</p>
<ul class="outs" style="margin-bottom:0">
<li class="out">A close that lands the same day every month &mdash; reconciliation happens weekly, not in a scramble.</li>
<li class="out">Bills paid on schedule &mdash; without you approving the same invoice twice.</li>
<li class="out">Invoices that get chased &mdash; without you making an awkward call at nine at night.</li>
<li class="out">A real number for the next bid &mdash; because the job costing is already done.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>

<section id="included" class="on-tan" style="padding:var(--pad-major) 0">
<div class="seg-in c-center">
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>How it works for Victoria contractors</p>
<h2 class="h2" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-5)">What working with Parallel actually looks like, month to month.</h2>
<p class="p" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-8)">Parallel does not run a portal with a login and a chat button. It runs a relationship &mdash; one CPA who already understands job costing, holdbacks and progress billing, working a fixed list of contractors across Vancouver Island.</p>
<div class="svcs">
<div class="svc-card">
<div><span class="svc-n">01</span><h3 class="h3">Monthly bookkeeping built around job costing</h3></div>
<div>
<p class="p">This is the recurring work. Every transaction gets coded to the right job, the bank and card feeds get reconciled weekly rather than once a month, and the close lands on the same day every month &mdash; not whenever someone finds the time. For a general contractor running three sites at once, that means the books reflect what is happening on the ground now, not what happened six weeks ago when someone finally sat down with the shoebox of receipts.</p>
<p class="p">Slow closes rarely start in the books. They start on site, in receipts nobody photographed and invoices nobody filed. Getting a new client&rsquo;s file current, usually in the first month, means catching that backlog before it becomes a habit rather than a recurring scramble every March.</p>
<h4 class="h4 svc-sub">Job costing that holds up</h4>
<p class="p">Retainage, holdbacks and progress billing are not add-ons here &mdash; they are the starting point. Parallel builds the chart of accounts around your jobs from day one, so a report showing true margin on a completed job takes minutes, not a weekend spent reconstructing it from memory and paper. That includes tracking what is owed back on a holdback, and when, so it does not get forgotten in a drawer until a job is long finished and the paperwork has gone cold.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="svc-card">
<div><span class="svc-n">02</span><h3 class="h3">Payables and receivables without the follow-up calls</h3></div>
<div>
<p class="p">Bills get paid on the schedule you set, coded to the right job and checked against what actually arrived, then queued for your approval once rather than twice. On the other side, invoices sitting unpaid get followed up on a schedule, so money owed on a Victoria job site does not quietly become money written off six months later.</p>
<p class="p">It is the call most owners put off until it gets awkward &mdash; the supplier chasing a late payment, the client sitting on an invoice past ninety days. Here it happens before it gets there, and it is Kevin making the call, not an app sending an automated reminder nobody reads. You still decide what goes out and what gets chased; you just stop being the one who has to remember it, or the one lying awake doing the math on who still owes what.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="svc-card">
<div><span class="svc-n">03</span><h3 class="h3">Payroll that lands on time, coded to the job</h3></div>
<div>
<p class="p">The crew gets paid on time, every time, and the deductions go where they are supposed to go. Hours come in from site, pay runs go out on the schedule you set, and the remittances are sent before anyone has to chase them.</p>
<p class="p">Wages are coded to the job they were worked on, so labour shows up in your job costing instead of in one lump at month end. For a Victoria contractor running more than one site, that is the difference between knowing which job made money and guessing.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="svc-card">
<div><span class="svc-n">04</span><h3 class="h3">Controllership, so someone senior owns the numbers</h3></div>
<div>
<p class="p">Someone has to own the numbers, not just enter them. This is the review layer over the books: the month-end close checked before you see it, the accounts that should tie actually tying, and statements you can hand to a bank or a bonding company without a second look.</p>
<p class="p">It is the work a full-time controller would do, at the hours a contractor this size actually needs.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="svc-card">
<div><span class="svc-n">05</span><h3 class="h3">A fractional CFO, without the CFO-sized bill</h3></div>
<div>
<p class="p">Past a certain size, the questions change. Not whether a transaction is coded correctly, but whether to take the next job, whether the business can carry two crews through a slow winter, whether it is time to hire a project manager instead of running every site yourself. That is the thinking this covers &mdash; usually quarterly, sometimes monthly in a busy year, always in language that does not need a follow-up call to translate.</p>
<h4 class="h4 svc-sub">A retainer, not a hire</h4>
<p class="p">Most contractors this size need the hours, not a title on an org chart. Call it a CFO on retainer &mdash; reviewing decisions before they happen, not producing a report after the fact that nobody has time to read. It scales down when the year is quiet and up when a big bid or a slow winter needs a second opinion.</p>
<p class="p">A lot of that thinking is the kind Kevin has already had to do himself, as a subcontractor working for other people&rsquo;s numbers before he started running his own. That is the difference between advice from a spreadsheet and advice from someone who has stood on the site while the invoice was still unpaid.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section style="padding:var(--pad-major) 0">
<div class="seg-in c-center">
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>From clients</p>
<div class="quotes">
<blockquote class="quote quote-card">
<p class="quote-t ph">Kevin had our payables off my desk inside a week. I stopped checking my inbox for supplier calls at ten at night.</p>
<cite class="quote-w ph">First name L.<span class="quote-r">Owner, general contractor, Victoria, BC</span></cite>
</blockquote>
<blockquote class="quote quote-card">
<p class="quote-t ph">He knew what a holdback was before I finished explaining it. Nobody else on the Island had asked the right questions that fast.</p>
<cite class="quote-w ph">First name D.<span class="quote-r">Owner, subcontracting firm, Vancouver Island</span></cite>
</blockquote>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<div class="cta on-dark cta-c">
<div class="cta-in">
<div>
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>No junior staff</p>
<h2 class="h2 cta-h">One CPA, CMA, who has actually worked a Victoria job site.</h2>
<p class="cta-p">Kevin Loo has been the subcontractor sending the invoice and the accountant chasing it down. Call and ask him anything &mdash; he answers his own phone.</p>
</div>
<div class="cta-act">
<a class="tel" href={site.tel}>{site.phone}</a>
<a class="btn btn-1" href={u('/contact/')}>Book a call</a>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<section class="on-tan" style="padding:var(--pad-major) 0">
<div class="seg-in c-center">
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>Getting started</p>
<h2 class="h2">Three steps, and the first one is a phone call.</h2>
<ol class="steps steps-3">
<li class="step"><span class="pt-n">01</span><p>A call. Fifteen minutes, mostly you talking about what is slowing you down.</p></li>
<li class="step"><span class="pt-n">02</span><p>A look at what you have now &mdash; QuickBooks, a shoebox of receipts, or nothing at all.</p></li>
<li class="step"><span class="pt-n">03</span><p>A scope and a price in writing, before anything moves.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="small muted" style="margin:0 auto">Rather talk first? Call <a class="tel" href={site.tel}>{site.phone}</a> and skip the form.</p>
</div>
</section>

<section style="padding:var(--pad-major) 0">
<div class="seg-in split">
<div>
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>Where we work</p>
<h2 class="h2" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-5)">Vancouver Island first. The rest of the province works too.</h2>
<p class="p">Parallel&rsquo;s desk sits at {site.address}. Contractors in James Bay and Fairfield drop receipts off in person; crews working out of Langford, Colwood and View Royal send everything over by phone instead.</p>
<p class="p">Most weeks that is a mix: a general contractor in Oak Bay, a framing crew based out of Saanich, a subcontractor bidding jobs from Nanaimo to Duncan. None of it changes how the books get run.</p>
</div>
<div class="area-box">
<p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-5)"><span class="mark"></span>Same CPA, same schedule</p>
<ul class="list-u">
<li>Downtown, James Bay and Fairfield: drop receipts off in person.</li>
<li>Langford, Colwood and View Royal: everything over by phone.</li>
<li>Sooke, the Saanich Peninsula and Sidney: reconciled weekly, the same way.</li>
<li>Up-Island and off-Island: delivered remotely, no waiting longer for an answer.</li>
</ul>
<p class="small" style="margin-top:var(--space-6)">Call <a class="tel" href={site.tel}>{site.phone}</a> &middot; <span class="ph">Mon&ndash;Fri, 8:30am&ndash;5:00pm PT</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="on-tan" style="padding:var(--pad-major) 0">
<div class="seg-in c-center">
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>Services in Victoria</p>
<h2 class="h2" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-5)">Other services available in Victoria.</h2>
<p class="p" style="margin-bottom:0">Need one piece rather than the whole back office? Each service has its own page for Victoria.</p>
<ul class="svl">
<li><a class="svl-a" href={u('/services/#bookkeeping')}><span class="svl-k">01</span><span class="svl-h">Bookkeeping</span><span class="svl-p">Monthly coding, weekly reconciliation, a close on the same day every month.</span><span class="svl-go">Bookkeeping in Victoria &rarr;</span></a></li>
<li><a class="svl-a" href={u('/services/#accounts-payable')}><span class="svl-k">02</span><span class="svl-h">Accounts payable</span><span class="svl-p">Bills coded to the right job and paid on the schedule you set.</span><span class="svl-go">Payables in Victoria &rarr;</span></a></li>
<li><a class="svl-a" href={u('/services/#accounts-receivable')}><span class="svl-k">03</span><span class="svl-h">Accounts receivable</span><span class="svl-p">Invoices followed up on a schedule, before they turn awkward.</span><span class="svl-go">Receivables in Victoria &rarr;</span></a></li>
<li><a class="svl-a" href={u('/services/#payroll')}><span class="svl-k">04</span><span class="svl-h">Payroll</span><span class="svl-p">Pay runs on your schedule, with wages coded to the job.</span><span class="svl-go">Payroll in Victoria &rarr;</span></a></li>
<li><a class="svl-a" href={u('/services/#controllership')}><span class="svl-k">05</span><span class="svl-h">Controllership</span><span class="svl-p">A senior review of the books and the month-end close.</span><span class="svl-go">Controllership in Victoria &rarr;</span></a></li>
<li><a class="svl-a" href={u('/services/#fractional-cfo')}><span class="svl-k">06</span><span class="svl-h">Fractional CFO</span><span class="svl-p">CFO-level thinking on a retainer, not a full-time hire.</span><span class="svl-go">Fractional CFO in Victoria &rarr;</span></a></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>

<section style="padding:var(--pad-major) 0">
<div class="seg-in split">
<div>
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>Questions</p>
<h2 class="h2">What Victoria owners ask before the first call.</h2>
</div>
<ul class="faq">
<li class="faq-i">
<button class="faq-q" type="button" aria-expanded="true">What happens when I call?<span class="faq-s" aria-hidden="true">&ndash;</span></button>
<p class="faq-a">You talk to Kevin. He asks what your books look like now and what is eating your week. That is the whole first call.</p>
</li>
<li class="faq-i">
<button class="faq-q" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Do you work with contractors outside Victoria proper?<span class="faq-s" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
<p class="faq-a faq-shut">Yes. Parallel serves Vancouver Island end to end &mdash; Langford to Sidney, Sooke to the Saanich Peninsula &mdash; and businesses further off the Island get the same service delivered remotely, on the same schedule.</p>
</li>
<li class="faq-i">
<button class="faq-q" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Can I get just one service, not everything?<span class="faq-s" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
<p class="faq-a faq-shut">Yes. Most clients start with bookkeeping or payables and add on later if it makes sense.</p>
</li>
<li class="faq-i">
<button class="faq-q" type="button" aria-expanded="false">What do I need to have ready?<span class="faq-s" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
<p class="faq-a faq-shut">Nothing. If you have last year&rsquo;s file and bank feed access, we move faster, but it is not a requirement.</p>
</li>
<li class="faq-i">
<button class="faq-q" type="button" aria-expanded="false">How is this different from an app?<span class="faq-s" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
<p class="faq-a faq-shut">An app gives you a login. This gives you a CPA who already knows what a holdback is and calls you back.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>

<section class="on-tan" style="padding:var(--pad-major) 0">
<div class="seg-in c-center contact-c">
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>Get in touch</p>
<h2 class="h2" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-8);max-width:var(--measure)">Talk to Kevin, or send the short version.</h2>
<div class="ct-grid">
<div class="ct-card">
<div class="nap-body">
<p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-4)"><span class="mark"></span>Find us</p>
<a class="tel-lg nap-tel" href={site.tel}>{site.phone}</a>
<ul class="rows">
<li class="row"><span class="row-k">Email</span><span class="row-v"><a href={site.mailto}>{site.email}</a></span></li>
<li class="row"><span class="row-k">Address</span><span class="row-v">{site.address}</span></li>
<li class="row"><span class="row-k">Hours</span><span class="row-v ph">Monday&ndash;Friday, 8:30am&ndash;5:00pm PT</span></li>
</ul>
<p class="btn-row" style="margin-top:var(--space-6)"><a class="btn-txt" href={site.directions} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Get directions</a></p>
</div>
<div class="nap-map ct-map has-map"><iframe src={site.mapEmbed} title="Map showing the Parallel Accounting office" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade"></iframe></div>
</div>
<form class="form ct-card" data-form method="post" action={site.formEndpoint}>
<FormMeta source="Victoria page" />
<p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-4)"><span class="mark"></span>Send the short version</p>
<div class="form-row">
<label class="field field-s"><span class="field-lab">Name</span><input class="field-in" name="name" required type="text"></label>
<label class="field field-s"><span class="field-lab">Phone</span><input class="field-in" name="phone" type="tel"></label>
</div>
<label class="field field-w"><span class="field-lab">Email</span><input class="field-in" name="email" required type="email"></label>
<label class="field field-w"><span class="field-lab">What is going on with your books?</span><textarea class="field-in field-ta" name="message" required></textarea></label>
<button class="btn btn-2" type="submit">Send it over</button>
<p class="form-note" data-form-note>Kevin reads these himself. Expect a reply within one business day.</p>
</form>
</div>
</div>
</section>
